ans need to be a  bit braver oh there's no question that  we're afraid I'm all the dreadful  dreadful grooming gang stories all those  many many convictions and for child  sexual exploitation in practically every  putt every part of the country that is  shown so vividly what happens when we  censor ourselves when we don't allow  ourselves to acknowledge that there may  be a particular dimension to a problem  so it's it does two things one is it  leaves those particular children exposed  for far too long to the particular home  but also it means we don't have the  right conversation about what the things  are that we need to tackle and how to  address them as as early as we possibly  can and yes sometimes sometimes like  very much affects girls but it cuts  across the board because we've got all  these different rights that can bump  into each other so we desperately need a  lot of these situations everybody to be  a bit bright braver and calling thing  by their name talking about what's there  I mean I guess another right that we're  talking about is gain lesbian rights as  well and you think of course of those  protests outside that school in  Birmingham about the teaching of  relationship education family education  do you think the schools are supported  enough in those cases I know that the  schools absolutely have felt unsupported  and we we leave the decisions to schools  about what to teach and when there is  there is some government guidance but  it's it's pretty high level there's an  in practice and immense responsibility  on the heads even of tighly primary  schools to take on something that is  really difficult and contentious in many  communities you say that schools full-on  supporter who should be supporting them  well the local authorities for Community  Schools the Maps local politicians  councilors MPs national politicians we  should recognize that there is a real  difficulty a real problem here that that  remarkably few people are willing to  pick up and talk about and we've been  talking about faith and and clearly the  majority of faith schools are doing a  great job men excellent job I'm sure  absolutely however there is a new wave  of state-funded voluntary aided faith  schools coming in and initially  announced by Theresa May do you think we  need to look at the number of faith  schools in the UK is no such general  argument that the off-state  Ofsted has about about faith schools  beyond that many of them do a fantastic  job if we look in the state sector  overall I think judgments for our faith  schools are at least as good if not if  not slightly better than also all  schools in the independent sector we see  a very different picture and we see us  of concerning picture among that that  relatively small group of schools but  it's what should be done about that then  the first step is acknowledging the  dimensions of the problem and saying we  do have we do now have a kind of  divergence among parent group parent  groups in different cultures different  backgrounds and that makes what we could  what we could have assumed everybody  would have accepted in a school 50 years  ago is no longer by default acceptable  to my  to nine parents out of 100 so we start  from further back in having to talk  about things in having to educate  parents in really having to rethink  models of Education to make sure that we  can take take take things forward and  build and unify people rather than  reinforcing divisions at the beginning  of the interview you mentioned the  report you have coming out on sex abuse  again a nun discussable topic for many  and it had some pretty worrying findings  what exactly does it show it shows a  number of things it shows first of all  that a lot of children are getting left  in the lurch for months if not years  through a very very long long drawn-out  investigations and this is when reports  are made about potential sex abuse yes  in in the home my child is disclosed  something that's happened to them at  home which of itself often takes a long  while for a child to be able to do and  then sometimes it can be weeks even  before the person is alleged to have  done whatever it is is even interviewed  it can take a year for investigations to  be completed in cases to come to court  and often during that time all the  attention is on the person being  investigated there's almost no attention  to the child and often arrest isn't used  bet there aren't bail conditions or  restrictions on the individual so the  child may be subject to the same abuse  throughout the investigation and then  even when people are convicted there's  often so very weak management of  offenders who come out of prison again  restrictions may not be set may not be  effective and there's a lot of weight  put on women to manage contact sometimes  with with partners or former partners  which puts a very heavy burden on them  especially when they might have been  abused themselves it's really concerning  and findings and there's been a lack of  join up as well for the problems that  children themselves may develop because  we find it so hard to talk about this me  nor mr. boo about sex between people who  are related and here we've got things  that are essentially manifestations of  incestuous incestuous relationships and  often child rape we find it so so hard  to talk about these things and call them  what they are and why do you think these  issues are arising is it partly funding  is it because  the agencies didn't have the money to do  the investigations in a timely fashion  or as you say is it more of a cultural  problem where we're scared to talk about  it I think in this one we've got  something that is very deep-seated in  probably in every culture around the  world that always has been a terrific  taboo around incest which makes it hard  to talk about these things nobody likes  to think of bad things like this  happening to to children in their own  home and yet the evidence says that  throughout history across the world  these things always have and always will  arise so it's a it's about being ready  ready to teach people to explain to  communicate that these things can happen  and joining up the services this this  lack of connection is something that  we've picked up in a number of the joint  inspections that we've done on on  dealing with domestic abuse child sexual  exploitation over and over again we see  this pattern of the different services  that are meant to help and protect  children finding it hard to talk to each  other hard to share information harm to  create this kind of concerted prompt  action and I just want to finish by  talking be about off stairs because  Ofsted is under pressure and teachers of  course often talk about the pressure  that Ofsted puts them under at the last  election Labour proposed abolishing  Ofsted entirely this is what the shadow  education secretary Angela Rayne has  said she said I believe Ofsted measures  poverty it measures deprivation it  doesn't measure excellence its Ofsted  biased against schools with more  deprived intakes it absolutely isn't  what we've said over and over again we  know that it's often harder running  schools in disadvantaged areas and yet  we're reporting to parents on what their  children are getting we could never say  that this education is is is good enough  for a child in Grimsby but it's not good  enough wouldn't be good enough for a  child in Notting Hill we have to keep  that same standard and I think if you  talk to parents everywhere they have the  same kinds of expectations for their  children no parent would say because  because we live here it's it's okay that  we should get a lesser standard of  Education so yes there are enormous  challenges in trying to make sure that  the system really can deliver the same  quality of education everywhere  but it's also right that the independent  Inspectorate should look at it and see  say what our children actually getting  talking about the importance of  inspections and there was a policy  previously where if a school had been  deemed outstanding then it didn't have  to go through these kind of automatic  timely inspection some schools weren't  reinstated for a decade that's now  changed was that mistake  it hasn't changed yet but but a very  welcome consultation has been published  on what was it a mistake to not have  these regular inspections outstanding  school our view is that it was it was a  mistake and we are delighted that  finally this this Education Secretary's  decided to do something about it because  it's left a lot of schools simply not  looked at and everybody knows that  schools that schools schools are not  honest or one-way conveyor schools can  get worse as well as better and of  course there's also much more to schools  than just the top-line reported results  so so leaving schools for 10-12 even 12  years even even longer um in my view has  been a really retrograde approach thank  you so much for being on the program and  talking so frankly about some of those  and difficult topics much appreciated  thank you

